  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Penny Sanderson handled the books and collected overdue payments when outdoor-equipment maker Sea to Summit was still a small operation. Its production team included only three machinists and Roland Tyson’s mother. Her work covered the practical administration needed alongside the design and making of products.\n\nTime away from the office became another part of product development. Sanderson and Tyson spent several months each year trekking, cycling, sailing and travelling overland, trying prototypes and refining designs along the way. She valued the fresh perspective gained on these trips, while the design team used wilderness journeys to discuss problems with equipment and test possible improvements.",
    "html": "<p>Penny Sanderson handled the books and collected overdue payments when outdoor-equipment maker Sea to Summit was still a small operation. Its production team included only three machinists and Roland Tyson’s mother. Her work covered the practical administration needed alongside the design and making of products.</p>\r\n<p>Time away from the office became another part of product development. Sanderson and Tyson spent several months each year trekking, cycling, sailing and travelling overland, trying prototypes and refining designs along the way. She valued the fresh perspective gained on these trips, while the design team used wilderness journeys to discuss problems with equipment and test possible improvements.</p>\r\n",
